9. THERMAL DE BROGLIE WAVELENGTH IN RAPIDITY
==============================================================================

  lambda_dB = h / sqrt(2 * pi * m * k_B * T)
  This is the quantum "size" of a particle at temperature T.
  When lambda_dB ~ interparticle spacing, quantum effects dominate.

  Particle     Temperature           lambda_dB (m)     lambda/l_P  phi(lambda/l_P)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  electron     Room temp (300 K)        4.3036e-09     2.6627e+26          30.4233
  electron     Liquid He (4 K)          3.7270e-08     2.3059e+27          31.5026
  electron     Sun surface (5778 K)     9.8062e-10     6.0672e+25          29.6838
  electron     Sun core (1.5e7 K)       1.9246e-11     1.1908e+24          27.7183

  proton       Room temp (300 K)        1.0043e-10     6.2139e+24          28.5444
  proton       Liquid He (4 K)          8.6978e-10     5.3814e+25          29.6238
  proton       Sun surface (5778 K)     2.2885e-11     1.4159e+24          27.8049
  proton       Sun core (1.5e7 K)       4.4915e-13     2.7790e+22          25.8395

  neutron      Room temp (300 K)        1.0036e-10     6.2097e+24          28.5441
  neutron      Liquid He (4 K)          8.6918e-10     5.3777e+25          29.6234
  neutron      Sun surface (5778 K)     2.2869e-11     1.4149e+24          27.8046
  neutron      Sun core (1.5e7 K)       4.4884e-13     2.7771e+22          25.8391

  Rapidity ratios at room temperature:
    lambda_e / lambda_p = 42.8500
    phi(lambda_e / lambda_p) = 1.8789
    This equals phi(sqrt(m_p/m_e)) = phi(m_p/m_e)/2 = 1.8789 (up to ln 2 factor)
    Exact: ln(sqrt(m_p/m_e))/2 = 1.8789

  Quantum degeneracy onset (lambda_dB ~ interparticle spacing):
    For electrons in metal: n ~ 10^28 m^-3, spacing ~ 10^-9.3 m
    lambda_e(300K) = 4.3036e-09 m (comparable! -> electrons are quantum at room temp)
    For atoms in gas: n ~ 10^25 m^-3, spacing ~ 10^-8.3 m
    lambda_proton(300K) = 1.0043e-10 m (much smaller -> classical)


